<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Genoese Famagusta</h1><p class="st-meta">1373 CE – 1464 CE · capital Famagusta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Genoese-held enclave around the great port of Famagusta in Cyprus, seized from the Lusignan kings in 1373 and held for ninety years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>At the coronation of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-cyprus">Peter II of Cyprus</a> in Famagusta in 1372, a dispute over precedence between Genoese and Venetian merchants led to riots in which Genoese were killed. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> sent a fleet in 1373, invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a> and occupied Famagusta, keeping the city as security for a huge indemnity.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> governed Famagusta through a podesta and officials, and from 1447 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-saint-george">Bank of Saint George</a>, which ran it as a business. The once-thriving port declined as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> restricted the trade of the rest of the island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Turkish pressure grew. Many inhabitants left and the economy shrank.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-cyprus">James II of Cyprus</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> support, besieged Famagusta and recovered it in 1464, reuniting the island under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">Lusignans</a>. Its Gothic cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a> and walls remain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1373 CE – 1464 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.02%;width:1.81%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1373 CE – 1464 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Famagusta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Genoese Ligurian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Famagusta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=genoese-famagusta" aria-label="Genoese Famagusta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5540 908 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Genoese Famagusta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5860 1164L5214 863M5860 1164L5845 1162"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5214 863h0M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5860 1164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5860 1164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Famagusta. <a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=genoese-famagusta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

